Here I am following up to my own post, and cross-posting to boot. 
Netiquette goes out the window.  But maybe getting this information into 
the Google archive will help somebody else someday.

While reading the Book of Ruth (OpenVMS Alpha Internals and Data 
Structures: Memory Management, by Ruth E. Goldenberg) I stumbled across 
the following tantalizing quote on p. 78

    "At boot time, the console determines the memory configuration and
     passes its findings as well as its own memory use to OpenVMS through
     a data structure called the hardware restart parameter block
     (HWRPB). ... Chapter Boostrap Processing provides more details on
     the HWRPB and associated structures."

I already knew about the HWRPB, of course, but the phrase "its [the 
console's] *OWN* memory use" was interesting.  The SRM console BIOS 
emulator must stash the results of these BIOS calls somewhere in the range 
of "its own memory use", so all I need to do is search through the range 
of addresses set aside for the console for a block of memory that looks 
like what I want.

Fortunately, the VESA standard specifies that the block returned by the 
0x4f00 BIOS call starts with the ASCII-encoded string 'VESA' followed by 
the version number 0x0300 (see p.25 of the vbe3.pdf document cited above). 
That gave me something to look for.

Furthermore, the console routine block (CRB) pointed to by HWRPB contains 
a virtual-physical map section.  The AARM describes the format in detail, 
but doesn't give any hint as to the purpose of these mappings.  I took an 
educated guess that the emulated x86 memory used by the BIOS_EMUL SRM 
callback must be located in one of these mappings.  The following program 
searches through the CRB mappings for the occurrance of the string 'VESA' 
and dumps some memory following it:

#include <hwrpbdef.h>
#include <starlet.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static char buf[256];

static const char *crb_search(const char *str)
{
     extern HWRPB *exe$gpq_hwrpb;
     HWRPB_CRB *crb;
     VAPAMAP *map;
     const char *ptr;
     long long i, len;

     for(len = 0; *str; str++, len++);
     str -= len;

     crb = (HWRPB_CRB *) ((long long)exe$gpq_hwrpb +
 	exe$gpq_hwrpb->hwrpb$iq_crb_offset);

     map = (VAPAMAP *) &crb->hwrpb_crb$iq_vapa_map;

     for(i=0; i<crb->hwrpb_crb$iq_map_count; i++) {
 	long long size = map[i].hwrpb_vapamap$iq_page_count *
 	    exe$gpq_hwrpb->hwrpb$iq_pagesize;
 	for(ptr = (char *) map[i].hwrpb_vapamap$iq_va;
 	    ptr < (char *) map[i].hwrpb_vapamap$iq_va + size - len + 1LL;
 	    ptr++) {
 		const char *p, *s;
 		for(p = ptr, s = str; *s && *s == *p; p++, s++);
 		if(!*s)
 		    goto found;
 	}
     }

     return 0;
found:
     for(i=0;i<sizeof(buf);i++)
 	buf[i] = ptr[i];
     return ptr;
}

int main()
{
     int arglist[2], i, *ptr, adr;

     arglist[0] = 1;
     arglist[1] = (int) "VESA";
     adr = sys$cmkrnl(crb_search, arglist);
     if(adr) {
 	ptr = (int *) buf;
 	for(i=0; i<8; i++)
 	    printf("%08x: %08x\n", adr+4*i, ptr[i]);
     }

     return 0;
}

and here's the output:

$ run crb_search
8b8622f5: 41534556
8b8622f9: 00000300
8b8622fd: 00010000
8b862301: 00000000
8b862305: 00000000
8b862309: 00000000
8b86230d: 00000000
8b862311: 00000000

0x41534556 is 'VESA' in ASCII on a little-endian machine.  The version 
code 0x0300 occupies the next two bytes.  I'm pretty sure I've found it. 
But there are still some strange things about it, such as the fact that 
it's not longword-aligned.

The reverse-engineering continues ....

