John Beardmore <wookie@wookie.demon.co.uk> writes:

> This machine has been unused for a couple of years.  Trying to load
> first Debian, then Gentoo it has been very prone to mystery go-slows,
> hanging, etc.
>
> I couldn't get it to complete a Gentoo 'emerge --sync' until I pulled
> out three of the four RAM cards.  These are very dirty, and although
> they were reliable when the machine was last used, I assume that the
> dirt that the fan has blown onto the leading edge of the card has
> decomposed to something a bit conductive.
>
> Last reboot I noticed an error message of the form
>
>      'unexpected interrupt 66 to CPU 0',
>
> and something about correctable RAM CRC errors.
>
> I had assumed that if the RAM is ECC, and all four cards are shown as
> 'passed' by SRM, that Linux would only be able to get access to
> reliable memory.

SRM does not test the memory automatically, it only checks how much is
present.  The SRM in the PWS series has a utility to test RAM, though
I never quite figured out the proper way to use it.

> Is this the case, or is Linux not implementing or using some aspect
> of the ECC mechanism ?

The checksums are checked by hardware on every read.  If there is a
failure, some sort of trap is generated.  Linux responds to this with
a kernel panic.  Tru64 kills the affected process and keeps running.

> Does Alpha Linux exclude memory pages which SRM knows to be bad ?

See above.

> I'll clean up the cards and try again, but I wonder if there is any
> documentation about how Linux uses the memory on this machine, and if
> ECC is fully exploited ?

There's always the source.

> Anybody know anything good for cleaning old decomposed dust out from
> between SMT chip pins ?

I'm sure there's something in a spray can available.

