I think you may be in for a sad suprise.  This is a BIG computer.  It
uses a lot of power no matter how you look at it.  I have used
AlphaServer 4000s before.  They are larger than a large air
conditioner.  Also, there is no internal bays (or at least I don't
think so) for hard drives.  That means in addition to the CPU unit, you
are getting another computer sized DEC RAID array box.  These things
are as loud as jet aircraft.  Also, it has a HUGE custom motherboard
with giant processor cards and RAM.  I doubt that you could put that
into another case.  Also, it is not the power supply that sucks power,
but the components themselves.  Running 10 SCSI drives and an
incredibly power hungry Alpha will definitely add to you electric bill,
maybe $500-$1000 a year, if it is running 24/7.  As for any dual
processor system, you need an identical processor card to run dual
processors.  Luckily there are not as many revisions/steppings as the
x86 stuff, but you still have to be careful.  Servers usually only take
ECC Ram.  Luckily, it is PC100 stuff, so it is pretty cheap.  As for an
IDE card, ALPHABIOS and the equivialent Unix BIOS are INCREDIBLY picky
as to what they will boot off of.  I don't know if any IDE card will
give you boot cabability, but you might be able to get away with a SCSI
boot drive and a large IDE disk that is recognized by the OS, but not
the BIOS.



