I've come across the more or less exact same thing trying to install on an
LX164. I think they must have forgotten to put down a BSD boot track or
something.

My trick is to use any other installation CD I happen to have lying around
(eg RedHat / SuSE, anything as long as it boots into aboot from the CD),
boot using an 'invalid' flags, eg

b dka600 -fl 9

This will cause the boot to stop at the aboot prompt.

Now switch to the debian cd, do a 'l' to see which kernel you'll need,
then just type the number for that kernel and off you go.

Happy Installs!


